evenge on its mind after the debacle of Munich. The 1976 US basketball team was coached by " [:a {:href "/athletes/2400499"} "Dean Smith"] " of the University of North Carolina, and he chose a team heavily laden with players from his own school – " [:a {:href "/athletes/6127"} "Phil Ford"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/5960"} "Walter Davis"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/6461"} "Mitch Kupchak"] ", and " [:a {:href "/athletes/6474"} "Tom LaGarde"] " – and rival schools in the Atlantic Coast Conference, including " [:a {:href "/athletes/7097"} "Steve Sheppard"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/5639"} "Tate Armstrong"] ". The two top European teams remained the Soviet Union and Yugoslavia, who had finished 1-2 at the 1970 World Championship (the USA was 5th), and the 1969 and 1971 European Championships."] "\n\n" [:p {} "But a re-match of the USA-USSR game never occurred. The United States almost lost an early round game to Puerto Rico, led by Marquette University star, " [:a {:href "/athletes/6503"} "Butch Lee"] ", but eventually prevailed, 95-94. In one semi-final, Yugoslavia defeated the Soviet Union, to set up a final game against the USA. In the final, the United States scored the first eight points, and was never challenged by Yugoslavia, winning 95-74. The USA team was led by Notre Dame star " [:a {:href "/athletes/5951"} "Adrian Dantley"] ", who averaged over 19 points per game."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button440315", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button440315').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {}] [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Team"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right", :data-original-title "Games Played", :class "split", :style "display: none"} "GP"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Points"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Scoring Average"] [:th {:data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right", :data-original-title "Time Played"